# 4. Fundamental product model from a UX point of view

The designer does not need to know the database, but must understand the domain objects because the UX must reflect them.

## 4.1 Atelier

The atelier is the main ownership space.

Everything belongs to an atelier context:

* pieces
* people
* molds/forms
* firings
* kilns
* failures
* production notes
* statistics

The app is not centered on one personal user. It is centered on the atelier’s production memory.

## 4.2 Piece

A Piece is one real physical object.

This is one of the most important rules in Vitrify.

If five identical cups are made, they are five physical pieces, even if the UI groups them visually.

Each piece can have its own:

* state
* origin
* purpose
* color/glaze information
* price/value
* failure record
* firing history
* commercial status
* notes

The UI may group similar pieces to reduce clutter, but it must never erase the fact that each piece exists individually.

## 4.3 Piece state

The core production states are intentionally simple:

1. To fire
2. To glaze
3. Ready

Failure is not a normal production stage.

Failure is a side outcome that can happen to a piece.

A failed piece may still need to remain in history for statistics, loss analysis, and traceability.

## 4.4 Piece purpose

A piece can exist for different reasons.

Core purposes:

* stock
* student/class work
* order/custom work

This is critical: Vitrify is not only mold-based.

Pieces may come from:

* atelier reusable molds/forms
* personal/student molds
* external or brought-in pieces
* hand-built or non-mold work
* experimental/test pieces
* one-off work

The UX must not force every piece to start from the main atelier mold library.

## 4.5 Mold/Form library

The mold/form library is the reusable atelier reference library.

It is useful for repeated production, capacity estimates, stock logic, loss statistics, and making new pieces faster.

But it is not the whole product.

The interface must support both:

* repeated production from known atelier forms
* one-off, external, student, personal, or experimental pieces

## 4.6 Person

A Person is a domain record inside the atelier.

A person may be:

* a student
* a client
* both

Do not design separate “student database” and “client database” experiences unless there is a very strong product reason.

The same person can take a class and place an order.

## 4.7 Kiln

A Kiln is a studio resource.

The kiln object should remain simple in the UX:

* name
* capacity profile
* active/inactive state
* notes or configuration if needed

The kiln is not a machine controller.

Vitrify records kiln use; it does not control kiln hardware.

## 4.8 Firing batch

A firing batch records a real kiln event.

It answers:

* which kiln was used
* what type of firing it was
* when it happened
* which pieces were loaded
* what notes were recorded
* what the result was
* which pieces advanced state
* which pieces failed or had issues

A firing batch has operational states such as:

* planned/loading
* active
* completed
* aborted

The exact labels may evolve, but the UX must preserve the distinction between loading, active firing/cooling, and completed history.

## 4.9 Failure record

A FailureRecord is attached to a piece when that piece fails or is lost.

Failure is important because it feeds real operational insight.

The UX must make it easy to record failure without making the user feel punished.

Failure data should support:

* reason
* category
* stage/context
* estimated loss value
* notes
* date/time
* relation to firing batch if relevant

When multiple pieces fail, the UI must support both:

* several pieces failing for the same reason and same value
* several pieces failing for different reasons and different values

The interface should not force one shared reason across many pieces if reality is more specific.

