# 5. Product principles

## 5.1 Physical truth over digital neatness

The interface must reflect physical reality.

Ceramic work is batch-like, messy, delayed, and retrospective.

The app must not assume perfect data entry at the exact moment something happens.

Users should be able to correct, backfill, and manually advance pieces when they forgot to use the app during real work.

## 5.2 Fast recording beats perfect forms

The app should minimize friction.

A studio user should be able to record common actions quickly:

* create pieces
* mark pieces as fired/glazed/ready
* start a firing
* complete a firing
* record failure
* adjust stock/commercial status
* find a piece

Avoid long forms when the action is common.

Use progressive disclosure.

## 5.3 The app should not become a generic business suite

Vitrify should not become:

* Shopify replacement
* CRM
* invoicing system
* booking platform
* accounting platform
* project management tool
* kiln controller
* education platform

It may integrate with or inform these areas later, but its core is atelier production memory.

## 5.4 Mobile-first does not mean mobile-only

The product must be excellent on phones and excellent on desktop.

Do not simply stretch the phone UI onto desktop.

Design two deliberate experiences:

* mobile: fast, task-focused, one-handed, vertical, operational
* desktop: wider, analytical, comparison-friendly, planning-friendly

The same product logic should power both, but the layout should not be identical.

## 5.5 Calm density

The app should show enough information to be useful without becoming visually crowded.

Avoid giant empty cards and oversized buttons.

Avoid toy-like UI.

The product should feel dense enough for professionals, but not like enterprise software.

## 5.6 Group visually, preserve individually

Identical or similar pieces can be grouped in list views.

But the user must always be able to inspect and edit the individual physical pieces.

A group is a display convenience, not a data truth.

## 5.7 Statistics must be operational, not vanity

Statistics should help the atelier make better decisions.

Avoid decorative charts that do not answer operational questions.

Good statistics include:

* production throughput
* pieces stuck in queue
* kiln usage
* failure rate by form/glaze/stage
* estimated value lost
* best-selling forms/glazes
* stock movement
* ready stock by category
* repeated bottlenecks
* production over time

Bad statistics include:

* decorative totals without action
* random pie charts
* “engagement” style metrics
* charts that look impressive but do not help the studio decide what to do

