# 7. Navigation model

## 7.1 Mobile navigation

Mobile must prioritize action.

Recommended bottom navigation:

* Bench
* Pieces
* Firings
* Stock
* More

The More area can contain:

* Statistics
* Library
* Journal
* Settings

If Statistics becomes a daily professional need, it may move into the main navigation later.

## 7.2 Desktop navigation

Desktop should use a left sidebar or compact rail.

Recommended desktop structure:

* Bench
* Pieces
* Firings
* Stock
* Statistics
* Library
* Journal
* Settings

Desktop can support split panes:

* list on the left, detail on the right
* filters on the side
* charts and tables side by side
* batch actions with persistent selection summary

## 7.3 Action placement

Primary actions should be obvious but not gigantic.

Core actions:

* New piece
* New firing
* Mark as fired/glazed/ready
* Mark failed
* Add note
* Search
* Filter

Actions should appear near the object they affect.

Avoid placing all actions in a global floating button if the action depends on context.

---

# 8. Visual design direction

## 8.1 Overall feel

The UI should feel like:

* an atelier ledger
* an inventory table
* a production bench
* a kiln log
* a calm professional tool

It should not feel like:

* a social app
* a beginner ceramics course
* a toy app
* a glossy SaaS landing page
* a bubbly habit tracker

## 8.2 Shape language

Avoid excessive rounded rectangles.

Use:

* flat sections
* dividers
* subtle borders
* rows
* tables
* compact cards only where useful
* restrained surfaces

Rounded corners, if used, should be subtle.

Avoid large pill buttons everywhere.

## 8.3 Density

The product should support professional density.

Mobile should still be readable, but not oversized.

Avoid “Duplo” UI where every button is enormous and every object sits in a large soft card.

## 8.4 Typography

Typography should be practical, readable, and slightly distinctive.

Recommended typographic behavior:

* clear hierarchy
* compact labels
* readable body text
* tabular numbers where useful
* no decorative type for critical information
* no tiny low-contrast labels

The interface must handle long object names without breaking.

## 8.5 Color

Color should communicate state and category without becoming decorative noise.

Color should be used for:

* production state
* warnings
* failure/loss
* ready/available
* active firing
* selected items
* filters

Avoid making every card a different color.

The base palette should feel calm, material, archival, and professional.

## 8.6 Icons

Icons may help recognition, but the app should not depend on cute illustrations.

Use icons sparingly.

Avoid generic craft icons unless they genuinely improve scanning.

Text labels should remain clear.

## 8.7 Motion

Motion should be functional only.

Use animation for:

* confirming an action
* opening a panel
* loading state
* selection feedback
* transition between list and detail

Do not use playful animation as decoration.

