# 9. Core screen specifications

## 9.1 Bench / Today screen

### Purpose

The Bench is the operational landing screen.

It should tell the user what is physically waiting in the studio.

### Main questions answered

* What needs firing?
* What needs glazing?
* What is ready?
* What is currently in a kiln?
* What has recently failed?
* What action should I take next?

### Mobile layout

Suggested mobile structure:

1. Compact header with atelier name and date/context
2. Quick action row
3. Active firing status, if any
4. Queue sections
5. Recent activity

Queue sections should show counts and the most important items first.

Example sections:

* To fire
* To glaze
* Ready
* Failed recently

Each section should allow tapping through to filtered Pieces.

### Desktop layout

Desktop can show:

* queues in columns
* active firing side panel
* recent activity panel
* quick statistics strip

### Important UX rules

* Do not turn the Bench into a chart dashboard.
* Do not overload it with lifetime totals.
* It should support quick action.
* It should feel current and operational.

---

## 9.2 New Piece flow

### Purpose

Create one or more physical pieces quickly.

### Required behavior

The flow must support:

* single piece creation
* quantity creation
* repeated production from existing atelier forms/molds
* one-off pieces not from the main library
* personal/student/external pieces
* multiple colors/glazes where applicable
* purpose selection
* optional person/order/class association
* editable price/value
* notes

### UX principle

The user should not be forced into a mold-first worldview.

The first decision should be about the origin/type of piece, not necessarily a mold.

### Recommended flow

Step 1: What are you recording?

Options:

* From atelier mold/form
* One-off or hand-built piece
* Student/personal piece
* External/brought-in piece
* Test/experiment

Step 2: Describe the piece

Depending on origin:

* select or type form/mold
* enter piece name/description
* quantity
* size category
* color/glaze information if known
* purpose

Step 3: Assign context

Optional:

* stock
* order
* class/student
* test/experiment
* notes

Step 4: Review and create

If quantity > 1, the UI should make clear that multiple physical pieces will be created.

### Important fields

* origin
* form/mold/name
* quantity
* color/glaze
* purpose
* person/order/class link
* price/value
* notes

### Bulk creation behavior

If five identical pieces are created, the UI can show “5 created” as a group.

But each piece must remain individually accessible later.

---

## 9.3 Pieces list

### Purpose

Show and manage all physical pieces.

### Required capabilities

* search
* filter
* sort
* group/ungroup toggle
* select one or many
* bulk actions
* inspect individual pieces
* edit piece
* delete with confirmation
* mark failed
* manually advance state

### Core filters

* state
* purpose
* origin
* form/mold
* color/glaze
* person
* kiln/firing history
* failed/not failed
* commercial status
* date created
* date updated

### Grouped view

Grouped view should group similar pieces by useful production identity, for example:

* form/mold
* color/glaze
* state
* purpose

Grouped rows should show:

* group name
* count
* state distribution if mixed
* warning if some pieces in group failed
* quick expand action

### Individual view

Individual rows should show:

* piece name/reference
* state
* origin/purpose
* color/glaze
* relevant person/order/class
* latest event
* warning/failure marker if any

### Selection behavior

Bulk selection is essential.

When pieces are selected, show a persistent action bar with:

* number selected
* change state
* assign to firing
* mark failed
* edit common fields if safe
* clear selection

### Important UX warning

Failed pieces inside groups must be visible.

Do not hide failed pieces inside a normal group count without a clear marker.

---

## 9.4 Piece detail screen

### Purpose

Show the full history and editable details of one physical piece.

### Required sections

1. Identity
2. Current state
3. Origin and purpose
4. Color/glaze/material information
5. Person/order/class context
6. Firing history
7. Failure/loss record if any
8. Notes/journal entries
9. Commercial/stock status
10. Audit/history timeline

### Required actions

* edit
* duplicate/record similar
* change state
* mark failed
* assign to firing
* remove from firing if allowed
* delete with confirmation

### Timeline

The timeline should show important events:

* created
* assigned to firing
* firing completed
* state changed
* marked failed
* edited
* sold/reserved/delivered if supported
* notes added

The timeline is important because Vitrify is an atelier memory system.

---

## 9.5 Manual state correction flow

### Purpose

Support real studio situations where the user forgot to record actions at the exact time.

### Required actions

The user must be able to manually mark selected pieces as:

* fired / moved to next production state
* glazed / ready for final firing or next state depending workflow
* ready
* failed

The app should not require every state change to happen through a full firing workflow.

### UX requirement

When manually changing state, ask for minimal context:

* date of change, default now
* optional note
* optional reason/context

Do not punish the user for backfilling.

---

## 9.6 Failure recording flow

### Purpose

Record loss or failure accurately without making the user fill repetitive forms unnecessarily.

### Core cases

Case A: one piece failed

* choose category/reason
* optional notes
* estimated loss value
* context/stage
* date

Case B: many pieces failed for the same reason

* select pieces
* choose shared failure details
* apply same reason/value to all
* allow per-piece adjustment before saving

Case C: many pieces failed for different reasons

* select pieces
* present per-piece rows
* allow reason/category/value per piece
* support copy-down from one row to others

### UX requirement

The flow should support both batch speed and individual accuracy.

Recommended pattern:

1. Select pieces
2. Choose “Mark failed”
3. Ask: “Same failure details for all?”
4. If yes: shared form with optional per-piece review
5. If no: per-piece editable table/list
6. Confirm

### Loss value

Estimated loss value belongs to the failure record.

Do not silently infer it from sale price unless clearly presented as a suggestion.

---

## 9.7 Firing creation / kiln loading flow

### Purpose

Create a real firing batch and load pieces progressively.

### Required behavior

The user should be able to:

* choose kiln
* choose firing type/context
* select/load pieces
* see approximate capacity
* save as loading/planned
* start firing
* complete firing
* record notes
* record failures/issues

### Flow structure

Step 1: Start firing batch

* kiln
* firing type
* date/time or planned schedule
* notes/program if relevant

Step 2: Load pieces

* show eligible pieces
* filter by state
* search
* group similar pieces
* add selected pieces to batch
* show loaded count/capacity estimate

Step 3: Review loaded batch

* list loaded groups
* expand individual pieces
* remove pieces before start
* warnings for mixed states or unusual items

Step 4: Start / mark active

* lock key details as needed
* show active/cooling status

Step 5: Complete firing

* review loaded pieces
* mark normal outcome
* record failures/issues
* add firing notes
* complete batch
* advance piece states only after completion

### Important UX rule

Pieces should not advance state when merely loaded.

They advance when the firing is completed.

### Capacity

Capacity should be approximate, not fake-precise.

Use simple categories:

* micro
* small
* medium
* large
* extra large
* irregular

The UX should communicate “approximate fit,” not exact packing geometry.

---

## 9.8 Active firing screen

### Purpose

Show what is currently in progress.

### Required information

* kiln
* firing type
* status
* start time/date
* expected cooling or completion note if available
* loaded pieces
* notes
* actions

### Required actions

* add note
* complete firing
* abort firing
* inspect loaded pieces

### Abort behavior

Aborting a firing is serious.

The UI should require strong confirmation and explain what happens to loaded pieces.

The user may need to record whether pieces failed, remained unchanged, or need manual review.

---

## 9.9 Firing history

### Purpose

Preserve kiln memory.

### Required capabilities

* list completed firings
* filter by kiln
* filter by firing type
* filter by date
* inspect loaded pieces
* inspect outcomes
* see notes
* see failures related to the firing

### Desktop opportunity

Desktop can show firing history as a table/logbook.

Mobile can show compact chronological entries.

---

## 9.10 Stock screen

### Purpose

Show what is ready and available.

### Required distinction

Stock is derived from physical pieces.

Stock is not a separate manual count detached from real objects.

### Required views

* grouped stock by form/color
* individual stock pieces
* reserved stock
* sold/delivered history if supported
* low stock / remake suggestions

### Core actions

* reserve
* mark sold/delivered
* move back to available if needed
* inspect pieces
* create similar pieces

### Important UX rule

Do not mix “in production” pieces into stock unless the user explicitly switches to a broader planning view.

---

## 9.11 Library screen

### Purpose

Manage reusable studio references.

### Sections

* forms/molds
* colors/glazes
* kilns
* size/capacity categories
* categories/tags
* default values

### Mold/Form library behavior

Each reusable form/mold record should show:

* name
* description
* size/capacity category
* default value/price if applicable
* active/inactive
* cast/creation count if tracked
* related statistics

### Important UX rule

The library should not become the only way to create pieces.

New pieces may be created without entering the main reusable library.

---

## 9.12 People screen or people access

People may not need to be a top-level nav item at first.

But the UX must support people records for:

* students
* clients
* both

A person detail should eventually show:

* linked pieces
* linked orders/classes if supported
* contact notes if relevant
* history

Avoid building a heavy CRM experience.

People exist to support atelier production context, not sales pipeline management.

---

## 9.13 Journal screen

### Purpose

Capture notes that do not belong only to one piece.

### Entry types

Possible journal entry types:

* general atelier note
* firing note
* glaze test
* production observation
* failure observation
* inventory note
* experiment

### UX requirements

* quick note entry
* date/time
* optional tags
* optional link to piece/form/firing/person
* searchable history

### Important rule

Journal entries should enrich production memory.

They should not replace structured records for pieces, firings, or failures.

