# 10. Statistics UX specification

## 10.1 Purpose of statistics

Statistics in Vitrify must help the atelier make operational decisions.

They should answer:

* What are we making?
* What is blocked?
* What breaks?
* What sells or moves?
* What should we produce more carefully?
* What should we stop making or redesign?
* How much value are we losing?
* Which kiln/firing/context creates problems?
* Which forms/colors/glazes perform best?

## 10.2 Statistics principles

Every statistic should have:

* a clear question
* a clear period
* clear filters
* clear relationship to physical pieces
* a possible action or interpretation

Avoid charts that exist only because the data is available.

## 10.3 Recommended statistics sections

### Production overview

* pieces created over time
* pieces completed over time
* current WIP by state
* average time in state if available
* queue aging

### Loss overview

* failed pieces count
* estimated loss value
* failure rate by form
* failure rate by color/glaze
* failure by stage/context
* failure by firing batch/kiln if relevant
* most common failure categories

### Kiln usage

* firings by kiln
* firing types over time
* pieces per firing
* approximate capacity usage
* failure rate by kiln/firing type

### Stock overview

* ready available stock
* stock by form/color
* reserved/sold/delivered if supported
* low stock
* slow stock

### Commercial movement, if supported

* best-selling forms
* best-selling colors/glazes
* sell-through by form/color
* stock value
* ready value vs lost value

### Form/mold insights

* most produced forms
* forms with highest loss
* forms with best movement
* forms with stock imbalance

### Glaze/color insights

* most used colors/glazes
* colors/glazes with highest failure/loss
* colors/glazes with best commercial movement

## 10.4 Statistics UI

Statistics should use:

* compact KPI strips
* tables for ranking
* simple line/bar charts when useful
* clear filters
* comparison periods where helpful
* drill-down from summary to actual pieces

Every chart should allow the user to inspect the underlying pieces or records where possible.

## 10.5 Mobile statistics

Mobile statistics should be short, stacked, and question-based.

Examples:

* “What is waiting now?”
* “What failed this month?”
* “Which forms lose the most value?”
* “Which colors are moving best?”

Avoid dense chart grids on mobile.

## 10.6 Desktop statistics

Desktop can support deeper analysis:

* tables with filters
* side-by-side charts
* drill-down panels
* export
* comparison views

Desktop is where planning and analysis can be richer.

---

# 11. Search and filtering

Search is central.

The user should be able to find records by:

* piece reference
* form/mold name
* color/glaze
* person
* firing batch
* note content
* status
* date

Filters must be easy to combine and clear.

Use filter chips or compact panels.

The user should always see when a list is filtered.

Provide a clear “clear filters” action.

---

# 12. Bulk actions

Bulk actions are critical because atelier work often happens in batches.

Supported bulk actions should include:

* assign selected pieces to firing
* change state
* mark failed
* assign purpose/context
* update commercial status if safe
* add note
* delete with strong confirmation

Bulk actions must clearly show:

* how many pieces are selected
* what will happen
* whether the action affects individual records

For destructive or irreversible actions, require confirmation.

---

# 13. Forms and input design

## 13.1 General form principles

Forms should be:

* short by default
* progressively expandable
* forgiving
* searchable where lists are long
* optimized for phone use
* friendly to retrospective entry

Avoid requiring every field upfront.

## 13.2 Autocomplete over dropdowns

For long or growing lists, use autocomplete/typeahead instead of dropdowns.

Examples:

* form/mold selection
* color/glaze selection
* person selection
* tags

The user should be able to create a missing item inline where appropriate.

## 13.3 Defaults

Use smart defaults carefully:

* quantity defaults to 1
* date defaults to now/today
* state defaults based on creation context
* price/value may be suggested but editable

Never hide important assumptions.

## 13.4 Required fields

Keep required fields minimal.

A physical studio record can be incomplete but still useful.

The user should be able to capture quickly and refine later.

---

# 14. Empty states

Empty states should help the user act.

Examples:

* No pieces to fire → explain that newly created pieces will appear here
* No active firing → offer to create a firing
* No stock → explain that ready available pieces appear here
* No statistics yet → explain what data will produce meaningful stats

Avoid cute illustrations.

Use plain, helpful language.

---

# 15. Error and confirmation behavior

## 15.1 Confirmation

Use confirmations for:

* delete
* abort firing
* mark many pieces failed
* destructive bulk changes
* irreversible state changes if they affect history

## 15.2 Undo

Where safe, provide undo.

Examples:

* accidental state change
* archive/remove from list
* simple edit

## 15.3 Error language

Error messages should be calm and practical.

They should explain:

* what failed
* what the user can do
* whether data was saved or not

Avoid technical jargon.

---

# 16. Desktop-specific UX

Desktop should not simply be stretched mobile.

Desktop should support:

* tables
* multi-column layouts
* split list/detail views
* advanced filters
* batch editing
* statistics exploration
* printable/exportable views
* keyboard shortcuts eventually

Examples:

* Pieces: table with filters and right-side detail drawer
* Firings: history table with selected firing detail panel
* Statistics: chart/table combinations with drill-down
* Stock: grouped inventory table with individual piece drawer

---

# 17. Mobile-specific UX

Mobile should support fast studio use.

Mobile should prioritize:

* large enough touch targets
* compact vertical lists
* sticky action bars
* clear selected states
* minimal typing
* quick filters
* scan-friendly rows
* short forms

Avoid:

* dense desktop tables
* huge decorative cards
* hidden critical actions
* long modal forms
* side-by-side layouts

Mobile is the primary operational surface.

---

# 18. Accessibility and usability

The product should support:

* readable contrast
* text scaling
* keyboard navigation on desktop
* clear focus states
* screen reader labels for controls
* touch targets suitable for studio use
* selectable text where useful
* no reliance on color alone for state

Because users may work in bright studio light, dusty environments, or with one hand, clarity matters more than visual novelty.

---

# 19. Localization and copy strategy

The UI should be designed with localization in mind.

Do not hardcode layout assumptions based on one language.

Labels may become longer or shorter depending on language.

The design should support:

* longer button text
* longer form labels
* localized date formats
* localized currency
* localized pluralization
* translated empty states and error messages

The design spec should define meaning and behavior, not depend on one specific language.

---

# 20. Data visibility and trust

The user must trust that Vitrify remembers correctly.

Design should make it easy to see:

* what was created
* when it changed
* who/what it is linked to
* which firing affected it
* why it failed
* whether it is ready/available/reserved/sold

History and traceability are part of the product value.

Do not hide important history behind obscure menus.

