# 24. Design acceptance criteria

A successful UX/UI design for Vitrify should satisfy these criteria:

## Product clarity

* It is obvious that Vitrify is for professional ceramic/porcelain atelier production.
* It does not look like ecommerce, CRM, booking, or accounting software.
* It does not look like a beginner hobby app.

## Workflow clarity

* The user can quickly create pieces.
* The user can see what needs firing/glazing/finishing.
* The user can create and complete a firing.
* The user can record failures accurately.
* The user can inspect individual physical objects.
* The user can understand stock separately from all pieces.

## Physical truth

* Grouping does not erase individual pieces.
* Failure is visible and traceable.
* Firing history is preserved.
* Manual corrections are possible.

## Mobile quality

* The mobile experience is not an afterthought.
* Main actions are reachable and clear.
* Lists are scannable.
* Forms are not exhausting.

## Desktop quality

* Desktop makes good use of width.
* Tables, filters, and details are efficient.
* Statistics and planning are stronger on desktop.

## Visual maturity

* The UI is calm and professional.
* It avoids excessive rounded cards and toy-like buttons.
* It has enough density for real work.
* It has clear hierarchy and readable typography.

## Future readiness

* The design can support deeper statistics later.
* The design can support more production contexts later.
* The design does not lock the product into being only mold-based.
* The design preserves the atelier-centered model.

---

# 25. Final product direction statement

Vitrify should become the professional memory and control surface of a ceramic atelier.

It should let the atelier see its physical production clearly:

* what exists
* what is waiting
* what is ready
* what failed
* what was fired
* what is in stock
* what should be made again
* what patterns are emerging

The best version of Vitrify is not a beautiful dashboard.

It is a practical, elegant, reliable atelier instrument.

It should feel like opening a clean production ledger that actually understands ceramic work.
